null},{"season":2024,"team":"CHI","distance":0.6374,"shared":15,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"DET","distance":0.6479,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2020,"team":"LAC","distance":0.6596,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Shane Steichen"},{"season":2025,"team":"MIN","distance":0.6643,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Kevin O'Connell"}],"defense":[{"season":2020,"team":"SF","distance":0.5398,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Robert Saleh"},{"season":2024,"team":"CIN","distance":0.558,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2018,"team":"MIN","distance":0.5668,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"JAX","distance":0.5734,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"PHI","distance":0.5871,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Jonathan Gannon"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Bills went 10-6 in 2019 and outscored opponents 314 to 259. The defense did most of the work, finishing 5th in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 20th in EPA per play. Their most distinctive traits were the 3rd highest empty formation usage and the highest Cover 4 rate in the league.","offense":"The Bills offense was a little below average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the 3rd highest empty formation usage and the 2nd highest no-huddle rate in the league. Josh Allen ran the passing game, with John Brown leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Bills defense was one of the best in the league by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the pass than the run, ranking 4th and 23rd. Jerry Hughes was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Jordan Phillips, who had 9.5 sacks despite playing only 53% of the snaps. Schematically, they had the highest Cover 4 rate in the league.","special":"The Bills were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 79% of field goals, which was a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the defensive line, about 26% of the money on the books. The offense also turned over more of its roster than most, with 32% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Josh Allen played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,089 yards. John Brown was the clear top target with 24% of the looks and 1,060 receiving yards. The backfield was a committee led by Frank Gore and Devin Singletary. On defense, Jordan Phillips led the team with 9.5 sacks and Tre'Davious White picked off 6 passes.","coaching":"Brian Daboll called the offense, and the defensive play caller for this season is not in the coaching data. Their offense looked most like the 2018 Cowboys and the 2024 Bears. Their defense looked most like the 2020 49ers under Robert Saleh and the 2024 Bengals under Lou Anarumo.","trends":"The offense went from 31st in EPA per play in 2018 to 20th in 2019, a big step forward. The defense moved from 3rd in EPA allowed per play in 2018 to 5th in 2019, about where it was. The biggest change in approach was 13 personnel usage, which went from 1% to 4%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2019. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}